[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Replace the transitions engine with a standard-library interpreter over the model document

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[WORK]
UID: WORK-SEMANTICS-STDLIB-INTERPRETER
TITLE: Replace the transitions engine with a standard-library interpreter over the model document
DEPTH: interface-settled
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Rewrite dev/scripts/sdoc_semantics/engine.py as a standard-library-only
interpreter over model.json and take the payload contract to
sdoc-semantics/2. What it does, in order:

1. Load and validate. Refuse a document whose schema string is unknown, whose
   operation names fall outside the closed table, whose state, gate,
   lifecycle, actor, checkpoint or transition references do not resolve, or
   whose lifecycle subject names a field or role the grammar does not declare
   (the grammar cross-check the spike already does). Errors name the
   vocabulary the document does declare.
2. Predicates. A closed table of operations over a node, its relations and
   the acting context: field_is, field_at_least (by ladder rank),
   has_relation (role, direction, optional target type), all_related (role,
   predicate), any_related, actor_in, and, or, not. About ten. A new operation
   is a small addition to the table with its own positive and negative test; a
   rule that fits an existing one is data only.
3. Dispatch. fire(graph, command, actor) preflights every gate on the
   transition, applies the write to a STAGED copy of the graph, collects the
   emitted events, ripples them through every lifecycle whose transitions
   listen (deterministic order: lifecycle order in the document, then subject
   uid), with a visited set and a step bound so a cyclic model refuses instead
   of hanging, then commits the staged copy in one step or refuses the whole
   change naming the gate or the bound that refused. The original graph is
   untouched on refusal.
4. Relation contracts. check(graph) reports every edge whose role, source
   type or target type breaks a contract, and every cycle on a role that
   admits none.
5. Diagnostics over the document alone: unreachable state, no initial, no
   terminal, ambiguous dispatch, grammar vocabulary mismatch. The existing
   positive controls carry over.
6. Rendering: a mermaid stateDiagram-v2 emitter with safe state identifiers
   (state names carry dashes), one diagram per lifecycle, gate names on the
   edges.
7. Payload sdoc-semantics/2: everything v1 carries, plus gates,
   relation_contracts, actors, commands, events, checkpoints, flows, and the
   derived gate placement (which checkpoints a gate may run at, from what each
   sees). by_type stays. The board (docs/sdoc/board/source.py, the Rules
   section, fsm.js) reads v2, and its unavailable degrade path stays.

The graph the interpreter reads is an adapter over the daemon's loaded corpus
(dev/scripts/sdoc_model.py), never a second parser. Nothing enforces on write
yet: the daemon does not call fire() in this item, so the operator's shaping
starts from a document with no gates and a writer that still accepts every
move.

Tests: every operation with a positive and a negative control; transaction
refusal leaves the graph unchanged; a cyclic fixture is refused by the bound;
load validation rejects a bogus operation and an unknown state name;
presentation order survives (never alphabetised); payload v2 keys are exact;
mermaid identifiers are safe; and the three shipped lifecycles produce exactly
the payload rows they produce today. Fixtures that exercise gates, ripple,
contracts and flows live under the tests only; the committed model.json gains
no gate.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Measurements this design leans on, from EV-SEMANTICS-ENGINE-ALTERNATIVES: a
standard-library interpreter covered every rubric row in 318 lines over a
250-line document; load and dispatch under a millisecond in-process; the
baseline's probe found an unreachable-state bug and a ripple that ignored one
event kind, both by negative controls.

Design calls made here, open to the operator: per-write gating in the daemon
rather than checkpoint-only, because scribe is the only writer; the write side
of a ripple is the interpreter's and rolls back as a whole; termination is
enforced by the interpreter, not assumed of the model; the board consumes the
same payload as the daemon.
<<<
